dict(pas="17.2", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^性/by nature | 相=xiāng/one another | 近/close | 也，/(assertion) | ^習/by what they are steeped in | 相=xiāng/one another | 逺=yuǎn/far | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="The Master said:\n“In nature, near to one another;\nin habit, far from one another.”",
  i="The Master said: “What people are born with keeps them close together; what they grow used to sets them far apart.”",
  n="Two clauses of one shape, each closed by 也, with no word to join them. 相 xiāng 'one another'; 逺 is the edition's form of 遠 yuǎn 'far'. 習 is the word of 1.1 'to rehearse', here what one is practised in until it becomes habit. Zhu Xi: the nature spoken of here includes the endowment of qi and substance (此所謂性兼氣質而言者也); natures so endowed certainly differ, finer and coarser, but taken at the start they are not far apart; practised in good one becomes good, practised in bad one becomes bad, and only then do they begin to be far apart (習於善則善習於惡則惡於是始相逺耳). He quotes Master Cheng: this speaks of the nature of qi and substance, not of the root of the nature; spoken of at its root, the nature simply is principle, and principle is never not good — Mencius' 'the nature is good' (孟子之言性善是也) — so how could there be any 'near'? He Yan's Kong Anguo draws the lesson: 君子慎所習也, the gentleman is careful what he practises."),
# ---------------- 17.3 ----------------
dict(pas="17.3", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^唯/only | 上/the highest | 知=zhì/in wisdom | 與=yǔ/and | 下/the lowest | 愚/in folly | 不/do not | 移。」=yí/shift",
  l="The Master said:\n“Only the highest in wisdom and the lowest in folly do not shift.”",
  i="The Master said: “Only the wisest of all and the most foolish of all are never moved from where they stand.”",
  n="唯 wéi 'only'. 上知 'the highest wisdom' and 下愚 'the lowest folly' stand for the people who have them; 知 is zhì 'wisdom' (Zhu Xi: 知去聲), and 與 yǔ 'and' joins the pair. 移 yí 'to move, to shift'. Zhu Xi: this follows on from the chapter before — within the general nearness of endowment there are also the fine and the coarse, fixed once for all, which practice cannot shift (此承上章而言人之氣質相近之中又有美惡一定而非習之所能移者). He quotes Master Cheng: the human nature is good at root, so why are some unmovable? Speak of the nature, and all are good; speak of capacity, and there is the unmovable lowest folly. It is of two kinds, those who do violence to themselves and those who throw themselves away (自暴自棄也). Anyone who takes himself in hand with the good can be moved; even the dullest can be ground and polished and advance by degrees; but the one rejects the good by not believing, the other cuts it off by not acting, and even a sage living beside them could not change them. Such men need not be dull: often they are stubborn and fierce, with talents beyond others' — Xin of Shang (商辛) was one; the sage calls them the lowest folly because they cut themselves off from the good, and, looking at where they end, they are indeed foolish. Zhu Xi adds that some say this chapter belongs with the last, the 子曰 being an intrusion (或曰此與上章當合為一子曰二字盖衍文耳). He Yan's Kong Anguo: the highest wisdom cannot be forced to do evil, and the lowest folly cannot be forced to be worthy."),
